{{nihongo|'''Shigetomo.'''|重朝。|Shigetomo}} is the 50th and final episode of the 1983 historical epic ([[Taisen drama]]) ''[[Honda Shigetomo (Taisen drama)|Honda Shigetomo]]''. Written by series co-creators Takafumi Miura and Yoshihara Kaneko, and directed by Takafumi Miura, it aired as a three-hour special presentation on December 19, 1983.

The finale covers the events of June 18, 1625, detailing the final confrontation between [[Honda Shigetomo]] (Shinobu Otake) and his childhood friend turned enemy, [[Ii Naotaka]] (Kōji Ishizaka) at the [[Battle of Ichijoudani]]. Deviating from traditional war dramas, the episode is noted for its slow pacing, psychological depth, and the extended use of silence. The narrative focuses on Shigetomo's recovery from the madness that plagued him in the "Winter Season" arc, culminating in a tragic gesture of reconciliation that leads to the "Double Suicide" of the two protagonists.

In [[Okaiken]], the episode achieved a viewership record of 90.4%, the highest-ever rating achieved by a Sanese television program, an event now referred to as the "Shigetomo Lock-in" where city streets were reportedly empty during the broadcast. It swept the 1983 Okaiken Screen Awards, winning Best Episode, Best Actor, and Best Director.

==Plot==
The episode is structured in three distinct acts, roughly one hour each.

===Act I: The Mud of Ichijoudani===
The episode opens ''in media res'' with the Honda army bogged down on the banks of the Ichijoudani River. Continuous torrential rain has turned the battlefield into a quagmire. Shigetomo, appearing gaunt and unhinged (retaining the "Otake Twitch" from previous episodes), orders a cavalry charge across the swollen river. The scene is brutal and chaotic; horses are swept away by the current, and [[Hotta Muneshige]] (Ken Takakura) is forced to physically restrain Shigetomo from riding into the water himself.

Following the failed assault, the Honda forces retreat to the dripping forests. Morale is nonexistent. A war council is held where Shigetomo drinks heavily, hallucinating that his generals are wearing funeral shrouds. Kanai Narukatsu (Imamiya Sadao) proposes a desperate plan to send a hostage to the Ii castle to negotiate, but Shigetomo dismisses it with a manic laugh, claiming, "Negotiation is for the living; we are already ghosts."

The turning point of the act occurs during a nighttime commando raid by the Ii clan. Shigetomo is cornered in his tent. His loyal bodyguard and lifelong servant, Kondo Tadakatsu, throws himself in front of a spear thrust meant for the Shogun. As Tadakatsu bleeds out, he holds Shigetomo's face and whispers, "My Lord... please wake up. The nightmare is over." The shock of Tadakatsu's death breaks Shigetomo's psychosis. The "Otake Twitch" ceases instantly, and for the first time in ten episodes, Shigetomo's eyes clear, signaling the return of his sanity.

===Act II: The Long Night===
The second hour is a slow-burn chamber drama set entirely within Shigetomo's tent. Shigetomo summons his second-in-command, Sakata Ishiemon (Sano Yuma), and asks for ink and paper. In a sequence lasting nearly twenty minutes with almost no dialogue, Shigetomo writes two letters. The audience watches him draft a threatening letter, which he crumples and burns, and a second letter offering the Ii clan the wealthy Bungo domain and full forgiveness, which he seals with his blood.

Shigetomo and Ishiemon share a final cup of sake. Shigetomo reflects on his childhood with Ii Naotaka, recalling their days as hostages under the Ogasawara. He delivers the episode's central philosophical monologue:
{{Quote|text="Look at the moon, Ishiemon. It is the same moon we saw in Yokosuka when we were boys. The world does not change; only we break ourselves against it. The Shogunate needs a foundation of stone, but I built it on the bones of my friends. To save the law, the lawgiver must die."|sign=Honda Shigetomo}}

When Ishiemon realizes Shigetomo intends to die, he draws his sword to stop him, threatening suicide if his lord leaves. Shigetomo gently disarms him, stating, "You must live to tell them why I died. A symbol is strongest when it is martyred."

===Act III: The Reconciliation and Death===
The final hour of the broadcast (aired without commercial interruption) begins at dawn on June 18. The torrential rain that characterized Act I has ceased, leaving the valley of [[Ichijoudani]] shrouded in a thick, freezing mist. Director Takafumi Miura utilizes wide, lingering shots of the fog-bound landscape, emphasizing the isolation of the two opposing forces. The soundtrack by [[Isao Tomita]] is completely absent during this sequence, relying solely on ambient sounds: the squelch of mud, the creak of leather armor, and the distant cawing of crows.

====The Silent March====
[[File:Honda Shigetomo Episode 50 scene 1.png|250px|thumb|right|Shigetomo (Shinobu Otake) walks into the mud during one of the episode scenes.]]

Contrary to popular legend that he went unarmed, the drama depicts Shigetomo donning full O-yoroi armor for his final approach, a historical correction based on the physical evidence held at the Okami National Museum. 

Actor [[Shinobu Otake]] portrays Shigetomo not as a mighty warrior, but as a man crushed by the weight of his own authority. The dark lacquered lamellar armor appears visibly burdensome on Otake's gaunt frame. His face, pale and unshaven, shows no trace of the manic energy from previous episodes; instead, there is only a terrifying, exhausted clarity.

He rides his horse slowly toward the castle main gate, stopping fifty meters away. The Ii clan archers on the ramparts tense, their bowstrings audibly creaking in the silence. [[Ii Naotaka]] (Kōji Ishizaka) appears above the gate. Ishizaka's performance is one of stoic restraint crumbling into devastation; he stares at his friend through the mist, his hand trembling on his sword hilt.

Shigetomo calls out, his voice cracking due to throat strain from his previous days of screaming. He uses Naotaka's childhood name, "Manchiyo," echoing their time as hostages in the spring season episodes. He feigns a final burst of the "Punisher's" rage, demanding Naotaka come out and face him, but the performance is hollow, intended only to draw his friend into the open.

====The Fatal Misunderstanding====
Seeing Naotaka hesitate, Shigetomo reaches into the breast of his heavy armor to retrieve the sealed letter of peace.

The movement is misinterpreted by a young, terrified Ii ''ashigaru'' on the wall, who believes Shigetomo is reaching for a hidden pistol. The soldier fires his arquebus. The shot is startlingly loud in the sound mix, breaking minutes of near-silence.

The camera zooms in on Shigetomo. The bullet strikes him in the upper right chest, punching through the lacquered scales of his breastplate with a sickening crack, sending splinters of metal and lacing flying. Shigetomo sways in the saddle, a look of profound confusion on his face, before slowly toppling into the deep mud.

====The Reunion in the Mud====
[[File:Honda Shigetomo Episode 50 climax.png|250px|thumb|right|Ii Naotaka (Kōji Ishizaka) deperately tending a dying Shigetomo.]]

Naotaka let out an unscripted, guttural scream and ran from the castle gate, unarmed, ignoring his own officers. He reaches Shigetomo, who is lying face up, drowning in his own blood.

The ensuing scene is regarded as the pinnacle of Otake and Ishizaka's on-screen partnership. Naotaka frantically tries to loosen the armor straps to tend to the wound. When he pulls back the heavy cuirass, the white silk underrobe (''shitagi'') beneath is already sodden with bright crimson blood.

Shigetomo, coughing blood, uses his remaining strength to shove the bloodstained, sealed letter into Naotaka's hands. Naotaka breaks the seal with trembling, muddied fingers. As he reads the offer of the Bungo domain and total forgiveness, Ishizaka portrays a complete psychological collapse, wailing incoherently as the realization hits him that the entire war—and his best friend's death—was a mistake born of silence.

Shigetomo grabs Naotaka's wrist, forcing him to make eye contact. Otake delivers the final lines in a wet, strained whisper, tears mixing with the blood on his face:
{{Quote|text="The true enemy... was my very own existence. Manchiyo... seal it."|sign=Shigetomo's last words}}
Shigetomo exhales and goes limp in the mud.

====The Double Suicide====
Adhering to the tragic ending of the ''[[Mitsukoji]]'' rather than the sanitized historical account, Naotaka refuses to leave the side of his dead lord. When [[Hotta Muneshige]] ([[Ken Takakura]]) arrives from the Honda lines, he finds Naotaka embracing the corpse, catatonic with grief.

Naotaka looks up at Muneshige. Ishizaka delivers his final line with deadened calm: "The sun cannot rise without the moon. Let us sleep in the same earth." He asks Muneshige to act as his second. Muneshige, the stoic warrior who had tried to prevent this outcome, silently draws his katana, tears streaming down his weathered face.

Naotaka kneels in the mud beside Shigetomo's armored body and commits ''seppuku''. The camera slowly pans up from the gruesome scene toward the grey, indifferent sky. The sound of Muneshige’s sword swinging is heard just as the screen cuts to black, ending the most ambitious television event in profound silence.

===Epilogue===
The episode concludes with a wordless montage set three days later. The soldiers of both the Ii and Honda armies—enemies just hours prior—are shown working side-by-side to build the funeral pyres. The final shot depicts the "Red String" ritual: a hemp rope is tied connecting the two grave markers. A sudden wind blows, causing the rope to sway, and the screen fades to white as the series' main theme, "River of Tears," plays for the final time.

==Production==
* '''Filming:''' The finale was filmed on location at the ruins of Ichijoudani in [[Fukui Prefecture]]. The production team waited three weeks for the perfect overcast weather to match the somber tone of the script. The mud in the battle scenes was created using 50 tons of imported soil mixed with water to ensure it looked historically accurate.
* '''Acting:''' Lead actor [[Shinobu Otake]] fasted for 48 hours and stayed awake for the night preceding the filming of the death scene to achieve a genuinely gaunt, exhausted appearance. His collapse in the mud was unscripted exhaustion that was kept in the final cut.
* '''Music:''' Composer [[Isao Tomita]] made the controversial decision to remove all background music for the final 20 minutes of the episode, relying solely on foley work (wind, footsteps, armor clanking) to heighten the realism.

==Reception==
"Shigetomo." is widely considered the greatest episode in the history of Sanese television.
* '''Cultural Impact:''' The 90.4% rating remains an unbroken record. The episode's focus on the failure of communication resonated deeply with the public. The phrase "Seal it" ({{nihongo|Fūjiro|封じろ}}) became a popular catchphrase in Okaiken business and politics, meaning to finish a difficult task at personal cost.
* '''Critical Analysis:''' Critics praised the subversion of the "glorious death" trope. Instead of dying in battle, Shigetomo dies by a senseless accident while trying to make peace, highlighting the tragedy of the era.
* '''Awards:''' The episode won every major category at the 1983 Television Academy Awards, and [[Shinobu Otake]] and [[Kōji Ishizaka]] were jointly awarded the "Special Achievement Prize" for their chemistry in the final scene.

